        "code": "import re\nfrom collections import Counter\n\nLOG = \"\"\"\\\nNov 12 03:14:07 web-01 sshd[2101]: Failed password for invalid user admin from 203.0.113.50 port 51234 ssh2\nNov 12 03:14:09 web-01 sshd[2101]: Failed password for root from 203.0.113.50 port 51236 ssh2\nNov 12 03:14:12 web-01 sshd[2103]: Failed password for root from 203.0.113.50 port 51240 ssh2\nNov 12 08:30:41 web-01 sshd[2188]: Accepted publickey for deploy from 198.51.100.7 port 40022 ssh2\nNov 12 09:02:10 web-01 sshd[2250]: Failed password for alice from 198.51.100.23 port 39900 ssh2\nNov 12 09:02:31 web-01 sshd[2250]: Accepted password for alice from 198.51.100.23 port 39900 ssh2\n\"\"\"\n\n# Compiled once, reused for every line. Named groups make the code self-documenting.\nFAILED = re.compile(\n    r\"Failed password for (?:invalid user )?(?P<user>\\S+) \"\n    r\"from (?P<ip>\\d{1,3}(?:\\.\\d{1,3}){3}) port \\d+\"\n)\nTHRESHOLD = 3          # failures from one address before raising an alert\n\nfailures = Counter()   # a dict specialised in counting\ntargets: dict[str, set[str]] = {}\n\nfor line in LOG.splitlines():\n    match = FAILED.search(line)\n    if match is None:              # the line is not a failed login\n        continue\n    ip = match[\"ip\"]\n    failures[ip] += 1\n    targets.setdefault(ip, set()).add(match[\"user\"])\n\nfor ip, count in failures.most_common():\n    status = \"BRUTE FORCE\" if count >= THRESHOLD else \"ok\"\n    print(f\"{ip:15} {count} failure(s)  users={sorted(targets[ip])}  {status}\")\n",
        "output": "203.0.113.50    3 failure(s)  users=['admin', 'root']  BRUTE FORCE\n198.51.100.23   1 failure(s)  users=['alice']  ok\n",
